1
00:00:02,800 --> 00:00:07,463
Seat four friends, A, B, C, D, round a table.

2
00:00:07,463 --> 00:00:11,656
In a row, there would be four factorial, twenty four orders.

3
00:00:11,656 --> 00:00:19,238
But round a table, rotate everyone one seat, and nothing has really changed: each person has the same neighbours.

4
00:00:19,466 --> 00:00:27,983
So the linear arrangements come in groups of four that are rotations of each other: four factorial over four, which is three factorial.

5
00:00:27,983 --> 00:00:33,426
In general, n objects in a circle: n minus one factorial.

6
00:00:33,426 --> 00:00:39,269
Another way to see it: fix one person's seat, then arrange the other n minus one.

7
00:00:40,900 --> 00:00:43,975
A garland or a necklace is different again.

8
00:00:43,975 --> 00:00:45,941
You can pick it up and turn it over.

9
00:00:45,941 --> 00:00:53,600
Clockwise and anticlockwise become the same object, so the count halves: n minus one factorial over two.

10
00:00:54,833 --> 00:00:56,479
A round table worked example.

11
00:00:56,700 --> 00:01:00,919
Eight in a circle: seven factorial, five thousand and forty.

12
00:01:01,133 --> 00:01:08,609
Tie the two friends together: seven units round the circle, six factorial, times two for their swap.

13
00:01:08,609 --> 00:01:10,499
One thousand four hundred and forty.

14
00:01:10,700 --> 00:01:14,957
Never together is everything else: three thousand six hundred.

15
00:01:15,166 --> 00:01:21,702
And a garland of eight flowers: seven factorial over two, two thousand five hundred and twenty.

16
00:01:23,733 --> 00:01:34,985
If objects can be reused, the choices never shrink: r places, n choices each, n to the power r. Post five letters into three boxes.

17
00:01:34,985 --> 00:01:39,216
Do not let the boxes choose; a box can take many letters.

18
00:01:39,216 --> 00:01:44,255
Let each letter choose its box: three choices, five times over.

19
00:01:44,255 --> 00:01:47,380
Three to the five, two hundred and forty three.

20
00:01:47,380 --> 00:01:48,416
Not five cubed.

21
00:01:49,633 --> 00:01:54,075
Here is the whole map so far, plus the one we meet next.

22
00:01:54,075 --> 00:01:57,577
Order matters with no repeats: n P r.

23
00:01:57,577 --> 00:02:03,606
With repeats: n to the r. Identical objects: divide by their factorials.

24
00:02:03,606 --> 00:02:08,385
A circle: n minus one factorial, halved for a garland.

25
00:02:08,385 --> 00:02:11,728
And when order does not matter at all: n C r.

26
00:02:14,133 --> 00:02:15,306
Pause and think.

27
00:02:15,306 --> 00:02:17,081
Six people, round table.

28
00:02:23,966 --> 00:02:27,692
One hundred and twenty: six minus one, factorial.

29
00:02:27,692 --> 00:02:36,181
Seven twenty is the row answer, and sixty is the garland answer, which only applies if the arrangement can be flipped over.

30
00:02:40,533 --> 00:02:43,451
Now selections, where order does not matter.

31
00:02:43,451 --> 00:02:47,188
Choose three letters from A, B, C, D.

32
00:02:47,188 --> 00:02:49,441
There are just four choices of trio.

33
00:02:49,441 --> 00:02:53,868
But each trio can be arranged in three factorial, six orders.

34
00:02:53,868 --> 00:02:58,196
Four times six is twenty four, which is exactly four P three.

35
00:02:59,400 --> 00:03:00,678
That works in general.

36
00:03:00,678 --> 00:03:14,766
Every arrangement is one selection followed by one ordering, so n C r times r factorial equals n P r. Divide, and n C r is n factorial over r factorial and n minus r factorial.

37
00:03:14,766 --> 00:03:20,592
To compute it, use the last form: r falling factors on top, r factorial below.

38
00:03:21,800 --> 00:03:22,833
A committee example.

39
00:03:23,066 --> 00:03:28,406
Exactly two women: choose two of four women, AND three of seven men.

40
00:03:28,406 --> 00:03:31,295
Six times thirty five: two hundred and ten.

41
00:03:31,500 --> 00:03:37,491
At least two women means two OR three OR four: separate cases, so add.

42
00:03:37,491 --> 00:03:38,517
Three hundred and one.

43
00:03:38,733 --> 00:03:45,523
At most one woman is everything else: four sixty two minus three oh one, one hundred and sixty one.

44
00:03:47,533 --> 00:03:49,227
A geometry favourite.

45
00:03:49,227 --> 00:03:52,313
Twelve points, five of them on one line.

46
00:03:52,313 --> 00:03:56,910
Any two points make a line: twelve C two, sixty six.

47
00:03:56,910 --> 00:04:02,014
But the five collinear points were counted as ten lines when they make just one.

48
00:04:02,014 --> 00:04:05,712
Subtract ten, add back one: fifty seven.

49
00:04:05,712 --> 00:04:11,415
Any three points make a triangle unless they are collinear: two hundred and twenty minus ten.

50
00:04:11,415 --> 00:04:18,722
And a twelve sided polygon: sixty six segments, minus twelve sides, leaves fifty four diagonals.

51
00:04:24,500 --> 00:04:25,713
The properties.

52
00:04:25,713 --> 00:04:30,277
There is one way to choose nothing, and one way to choose everything.

53
00:04:30,500 --> 00:04:37,220
Choosing r things to take is the same act as choosing the n minus r things to leave behind.

54
00:04:37,220 --> 00:04:41,369
So n C r equals n C n minus r.

55
00:04:41,600 --> 00:04:48,178
So if n C r equals n C s, either r equals s, or r plus s equals n.

56
00:04:48,400 --> 00:04:50,784
Pascal's rule, the important one.

57
00:04:50,784 --> 00:04:54,847
To choose r from n plus one objects, mark one object.

58
00:04:54,847 --> 00:05:02,890
Either you leave it out, n C r ways, or you include it and choose r minus one more, n C r minus one ways.

59
00:05:03,100 --> 00:05:12,324
And r times n C r equals n times n minus one C r minus one: both count choosing a committee of r and then its chairperson.

60
00:05:13,933 --> 00:05:17,125
Pascal's rule builds a famous triangle.

61
00:05:17,125 --> 00:05:23,778
Each row lists n C zero up to n C n, and every entry is the sum of the two above it.

62
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:26,632
Look: ten plus ten makes twenty.

63
00:05:26,632 --> 00:05:31,267
Five C two plus five C three equals six C three.

64
00:05:31,267 --> 00:05:41,449
The rows also add up to powers of two, one, two, four, eight, sixteen: the total number of subsets, which we will prove in the next unit.

65
00:05:42,666 --> 00:05:45,155
Three consecutive combinations.

66
00:05:45,155 --> 00:05:49,085
Take ratios: consecutive ones have a very simple ratio.

67
00:05:49,300 --> 00:05:59,559
n C r over n C r minus one is n minus r plus one over r. Set it equal to eighty four over thirty six, seven thirds.

68
00:05:59,766 --> 00:06:05,016
The next ratio is n minus r over r plus one, equal to three halves.

69
00:06:05,233 --> 00:06:08,724
Solve the pair: n is nine and r is three.

70
00:06:08,724 --> 00:06:15,167
Check: nine C two, three and four are thirty six, eighty four and one twenty six.

71
00:06:17,200 --> 00:06:17,703
Two more.

72
00:06:18,433 --> 00:06:26,130
Write each as falling factors over a factorial, cancel, and you get four times two n minus one over three.

73
00:06:26,130 --> 00:06:29,931
Equal to forty four thirds: n is six.

74
00:06:30,133 --> 00:06:36,741
If n C eight equals n C twelve, and eight is not twelve, then n is twenty.

75
00:06:36,966 --> 00:06:40,327
And never compute twenty C seventeen directly.

76
00:06:40,327 --> 00:06:47,508
Use twenty C three: twenty times nineteen times eighteen over six, one thousand one hundred and forty.

77
00:06:50,133 --> 00:06:51,541
Exam tip.

78
00:06:51,541 --> 00:06:54,431
Never multiply out big factorials.

79
00:06:54,431 --> 00:07:04,180
Cancel first, and always use the smaller of r and n minus r. Fifty C forty eight is just fifty C two, twelve twenty five.

80
00:07:06,166 --> 00:07:07,379
Pause and think.

81
00:07:07,379 --> 00:07:10,777
n C nine equals n C seven.

82
00:07:10,777 --> 00:07:11,314
What is n?

83
00:07:18,200 --> 00:07:19,204
Sixteen.

84
00:07:19,204 --> 00:07:25,793
Since nine is not seven, the property says nine plus seven must equal n.

85
00:07:25,793 --> 00:07:27,994
Two is the trap from subtracting.

86
00:07:29,966 --> 00:07:30,814
One more.

87
00:07:30,814 --> 00:07:31,187
LEVEL.

88
00:07:38,066 --> 00:07:38,888
Thirty.

89
00:07:38,888 --> 00:07:45,698
Five letters, with L twice and E twice: one twenty over two times two.

90
00:07:45,698 --> 00:07:49,401
One twenty is the trap: it ignores the repeated letters.

91
00:07:51,600 --> 00:07:52,682
Quick recap.

92
00:07:52,682 --> 00:07:55,404
OR adds and AND multiplies.

93
00:07:55,404 --> 00:08:02,083
Permutations, where order matters, are n factorial over n minus r factorial.

94
00:08:02,083 --> 00:08:08,789
Fill restricted places first, tie blocks for together, and use gaps for no two together.

95
00:08:08,789 --> 00:08:16,432
Repeated objects divide by their factorials; circles give n minus one factorial, halved for a garland.

96
00:08:16,432 --> 00:08:20,352
Combinations are permutations divided by r factorial.

97
00:08:20,352 --> 00:08:24,994
And Pascal's rule builds the triangle whose rows add up to powers of two.

98
00:08:29,166 --> 00:08:31,733
That was permutation and combination.

99
00:08:31,733 --> 00:08:39,259
Next, the binomial theorem, where those same n C r numbers turn up as the coefficients of every expansion.

100
00:08:39,259 --> 00:08:39,821
See you there.

